      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "mode",
      "value": "code_lab"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Test the Curvature-Induced Rigidity Hypothesis by constructing a constrained optimization manifold with tunable Gaussian curvature, measuring solution space rigidity via Hessian spectrum analysis and geodesic distance stability. Generate synthetic data on embedded manifolds (spherical/flat/hyperbolic) with curvature κ ∈ {-1, 0, 1}, train geometry-aware networks with curvature-regularized losses, and compute N_c(w) as the minimum width achieving stable geodesic reconstruction (error ≤ 1e-3) across w ∈ {10, 50, 100, 500}. Rigidity is confirmed if N_c(w) remains bounded (≤ 2× baseline) under increasing curvature and sample size, while fat solution spaces show unbounded N_c(w) growth."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "For positively curved manifolds (κ>0), N_c(w) will remain bounded (rigid solution space); for flat/hyperbolic (κ≤0), N_c(w) will scale with √w or linearly (fat solution space), demonstrating curvature-induced geometric constraint on solution capacity."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "prediction_inputs"
    },
